 1 big thing: House Republicans launch Alaska probe 
 
 
 &nbsp; 
 
 Illustration: Maura Losch/Axios House Oversight Committee Chair James Comer (R-Ky.) is investigating allegations that Democratic operatives recruited an Alaska Senate candidate with the same name as incumbent Dan Sullivan (R) in a bid to siphon votes from him, Axios' Kate Santaliz reports. 

 The probe draws Congress into a controversy surrounding Democratic Senate candidate Mary Peltola's campaign in one of the country's most competitive midterm races. 🔎 Comer is seeking transcribed interviews with Peltola's former campaign manager, Elisa Rios, as well as Democratic operative Jerid Kurtz.

 He threatened to use subpoenas to compel testimony from the two Democratic operatives, who were allegedly involved in the recruitment effort. Neither Rios nor Kurtz responded to Axios' requests for comment. Driving the news: The New York Times reported yesterday that top Peltola campaign staffers directed efforts to track down another Dan Sullivan to enter the race as part of a bid to recruit potential spoilers.

 The other Dan Sullivan, a retired teacher who will also appear on the ballot, registered as a Republican shortly before the filing deadline. Early voting yesterday in Anchorage, Alaska. Photo: Nathaniel Wilder/Bloomberg via Getty Images Both the Peltola campaign and the Democratic Senatorial Campaign Committee denied involvement in recruiting the other Sullivan, according to the Times.

 Neither responded to Axios' request for comment. Rios, who was fired amid an August staff upheaval, told the Times that "voters should get the full picture when making their decisions." The newspaper said she later tried to retract her comments for the story. Go deeper . 

 
 
